## Interface the tool layer calls (crate mixdog-office, `mixdog_office::author`)
- `author(&H, args: &Value, &AuthorCall { cwd, signal }) -> Result<Value, AuthorError>` (async; `H: AuthorHost`) is `authorAction` of
  `core/office-sessionless-actions.mjs` without its result wrapper: the write receipts of the document and of the sidecars
  (`<doc>.mixdog-source.html`, `<doc>.mixdog-html.pdf`) go to `AuthorHost::note_write` first, then the route the extension names
  (`.pdf`, `.docx`/`.xlsx`, anything else a deck). The answer is Node's object in Node's key order; its `_images` (pixels of the render) are
  what the dispatch attaches to the tool result and deletes from the object, and `finalizeOfficeResult(authored, { action: 'author',
  session: registry.get(authored.session), startedAt })` stays the dispatch's. `AuthorError::Abort` is Node's `AbortError`, `Failed` any
  other thrown error (the tool result is `isError` with its message in both cases).
- `AuthorHost: StageHost` is what the action asks of the machine: files (`exists`, `read_text`, `make_directory` = mkdir + mkdtemp,
  `copy_file`, `remove_file`, `remove_dir_all`), the registry (`session_for_document`, `release_session`, `close_session`), the Office
  host (`office_call`), session creation (`create_authored_session`, `register_html_pdf_session`, `release_overwritten_session`), the
  readers (`snapshot_deck`, `snapshot_pdf_pages`), the runners (`run_pptx_script`, `run_pptx_html` with `frame` for a PDF's designed
  pages, `run_docx_html`, `run_xlsx_html`, `print_pdf`), the review (`inline_audit`, `render`, `read_composition_receipt`,
  `receipt_for_delivery`) and the comparison images (`write_html_comparisons`, `measure_html_drift`, `read_bytes`, `write_bytes`,
  `compose_comparison`).
- `SystemAuthor { env: SessionEnv, inspect: &InspectContext, render: &R: RenderEngine, closer: &C: SessionCloser, endpoint: &dyn
  BrowserEndpoint, lanes: LaneTokens, fonts: Arc<Registry>, svg_fonts: &SvgFonts, note: Arc<dyn WriteNote>, deliveries }` is the
  host of a process (`deliveries: Mutex::default()`). `closer` is the finalize stage's `closeSession` (nr-office-engine-qa-finalize-dispatch
  implements `SessionCloser`); the render receipt's memory of what a session was last sent (`DeliveredReceipt`) lives in `deliveries`,
  keyed by session id, and `release_session`'s caller (the close of a session) should `deliveries.lock().remove(id)`.
- `author::pptx_session`: `staging_target`, `release_staging_target`, `land_staged_deck`, `swap_authored_document`,
  `reusable_authored_session`, `release_existing_session`, `throw_if_authoring_cancelled`, `AuthorError`, `STAGING_PREFIX`;
  `author::pptx`: `author_pptx`, `gate_staged_deck`, `plan_gate_result`, `facts_gate_result`, `geometry_gate_result`;
  `author::pdf::author_pdf`; `author::html_document::{author_html_document, comparison_sheet}`.

## The pptxgenjs script route (nr-office-author-script-runtime, wave 99)
A source that does not open with `<` is a pptxgenjs script. `SystemAuthor::run_pptx_script` answers the reason once as the error of the call
("the script runtime is not part of this build ... author the deck as HTML slides") until nr-office-author-script-runtime exists; that task
replaces the body of that one method with its runtime (`run_pptx_authoring_script(script, output, cwd)`, answering Node's run object: `ok`,
`output`, `bytes`, `logs`, `kit`, `elapsedMs`, or `ok: false` with `error`, `logs`, `elapsedMs`) and nothing else in this module changes. No
other implementation of the route is kept.

## What was not compiled or run
Nothing was compiled or run in Rust. The first compile of `author/` (five modules and `system.rs`, which names many engine types: the
lifetimes of `SessionEnv` in `SystemAuthor::session_env` and the `Prepare` closure of `run_pptx_html` are the places most likely to need a
touch) and of `tests/twin_author.rs` is the lead's. `twin_live_author` (ignored) re-records through Node and was not run in Rust; the
recorder itself was run once (Node only, no browser or Office) and produced the committed recording.
